  <p>
    Moving to the UK means navigating one of the world's more complex immigration systems.
    The good news is that there are several well-established routes into London depending on
    your situation — whether you have a job offer, you're a recent graduate, you're joining a
    partner, or you're eligible for a working holiday scheme. Here's an overview of the most
    common paths.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Common routes ────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Common visa routes at a glance</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skilled Worker Visa</strong> — you have a job offer from a licensed UK employer</li>
    <li><strong>Youth Mobility Scheme</strong> — you're under 31 and from an eligible country</li>
    <li><strong>Graduate Visa</strong> — you just finished a degree at a UK university</li>
    <li><strong>Student Visa</strong> — you've been offered a place at a UK university</li>
    <li><strong>Family Visa</strong> — you're joining a UK citizen or settled person</li>
    <li><strong>EU Settlement Scheme</strong> — you were living in the UK before December 2020</li>
    <li><strong>Global Talent Visa</strong> — you're recognised as a leader in your field</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ── Skilled Worker ────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Skilled Worker Visa</h2>

  <p>
    The most common route for people moving to London for work. To be eligible, you need:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A confirmed <strong>job offer from a UK employer</strong> who holds a valid sponsor licence</li>
    <li>The role must meet a minimum <strong>skill level</strong> (RQF Level 3 or above — roughly A-level equivalent)</li>
    <li>Your salary must meet the <strong>minimum salary threshold</strong> for your role — check the current figure on gov.uk, as this changes</li>
    <li>You must meet an <strong>English language requirement</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The visa is typically granted for up to <strong>5 years</strong> and can be extended.
    After 5 years of continuous residence, you may be eligible to apply for
    <strong>Indefinite Leave to Remain (ILR)</strong> — effectively permanent residency.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Tip</span>
    <p>
      Your employer arranges the sponsorship — but it's worth checking that any prospective
      employer holds a valid sponsor licence before accepting a job offer. You can
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/check-uk-visa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">search the register of licensed sponsors on gov.uk</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Youth Mobility ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Youth Mobility Scheme</h2>

  <p>
    One of the most flexible routes into the UK — and often the easiest entry point for young
    internationals. If you're a citizen of an <strong>eligible country</strong> and aged
    between <strong>18 and 30</strong> (some nationalities up to 35), you can live and work in
    the UK for up to <strong>2 years</strong> without needing a job offer first.
  </p>

  <p>
    Eligible countries as of publication include: Australia, Canada, New Zealand, Japan,
    South Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong (BN(O) holders), Monaco, San Marino, Uruguay and Andorra —
    with more being added over time. Check
    <a href="https://www.gov.uk/youth-mobility" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gov.uk/youth-mobility</a>
    for the current full list and age limits by nationality.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">Worth knowing</span>
    <p>
      The Youth Mobility Scheme is a <strong>non-extendable</strong> route — you can't switch
      into it from within the UK, and you can't extend it when it expires. Many people use this
      time to find an employer willing to sponsor a Skilled Worker Visa, giving them long-term
      options.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Graduate Visa ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Graduate Visa</h2>

  <p>
    If you've just completed a degree (bachelor's, master's or PhD) at a recognised UK
    university, the Graduate Visa lets you stay and work in the UK for
    <strong>2 years</strong> after graduating (3 years for PhD graduates).
    You can work in any job at any salary level — there's no employer sponsorship required.
  </p>

  <p>
    You must apply <strong>before your Student Visa expires</strong>. The Graduate Visa itself
    cannot be extended, but it gives you a window to find a Skilled Worker sponsor if you want
    to stay longer.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Student Visa ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Student Visa</h2>

  <p>
    To study in the UK at degree level or above, you'll need a Student Visa. You'll need a
    <strong>Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies (CAS)</strong> from a UK university or
    college that holds a sponsor licence, proof of English language proficiency, and evidence
    of sufficient funds to support yourself during your studies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most students can work <strong>up to 20 hours per week</strong> during term time and
    full-time during official holidays. You cannot run your own business on a Student Visa.
  </p>

  <!-- ── EU Settlement ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>EU Settlement Scheme</h2>

  <p>
    If you're an EU, EEA or Swiss citizen who was living in the UK <strong>before
    31 December 2020</strong>, you should have applied (or be able to apply with a valid
    reason for lateness) for either:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Settled status</strong> — if you had been in the UK for 5 or more continuous years</li>
    <li><strong>Pre-settled status</strong> — if you had been in the UK for less than 5 years</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you have pre-settled status, you'll eventually need to apply to upgrade to settled
    status. The scheme is now closed to new applicants (with limited exceptions) — if you
    arrived after December 2020, you'll need a different visa route.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--official">
    <span class="callout__heading">Check your EU Settlement Scheme status</span>
    <ul class="official-links">
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.gov.uk/settled-status-eu-citizens-families" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          EU Settlement Scheme — gov.uk
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Family Visa ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Family Visa</h2>

  <p>
    If you're joining a <strong>UK citizen or a person with settled status</strong> as a
    spouse, civil partner or long-term partner, you may be eligible for a Family Visa. The
    person in the UK (the sponsor) must meet a minimum income requirement — the current
    threshold is on gov.uk and has changed in recent years, so verify before applying.
  </p>

  <p>
    English language requirements apply. The initial visa is usually granted for
    2.5 years, with a further 2.5-year extension before you can apply for ILR.
  </p>

  <!-- ── When you arrive ─────────────────── -->
  <h2>When you arrive: what to do</h2>

  <p>
    Most visa holders will need to collect a <strong>Biometric Residence Permit (BRP)</strong>
    from a designated Post Office within 10 days of arriving in the UK. Your visa or entry
    clearance will state where to collect it. Don't miss this — it's your main proof of right
    to work and rent.
  </p>

  <p>
    You'll also have paid (or need to pay) the
    <strong>Immigration Health Surcharge (IHS)</strong> as part of your application —
    this gives you access to NHS services during your visa period.
    The current rate is on gov.uk and varies by visa type and length.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Your first week checklist</span>
    <ul>
      <li>Collect your BRP from the Post Office listed in your vignette</li>
      <li>Open a UK bank account (see our <a href="/anchor/banking">Banking guide</a>)</li>
      <li>Register with a GP — you're entitled to NHS care once the IHS is paid</li>
      <li>If you're working, make sure your employer has completed a Right to Work check</li>
      <li>Register on the electoral roll if you're eligible — it helps your credit score</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Extensions ─────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Extending your visa</h2>

  <p>
    Most visas can be extended from within the UK. As a general rule, apply well before
    your current visa expires — ideally <strong>at least 3 months in advance</strong>.
    You can usually continue in your current role while an extension is pending under
    <strong>"section 3C leave"</strong>, which automatically extends your right to stay
    and work while the Home Office processes your application.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not let your visa expire without applying for an extension or a new visa. Overstaying
    has serious consequences including bans on future applications.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Where to get help ──────────────── -->
  <h2>Where to get proper help</h2>

  <p>
    In the UK, only <strong>OISC-registered advisers</strong> or solicitors regulated by the
    Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA) are legally permitted to give immigration advice for
    a fee. Be very cautious of anyone else who offers to help with your visa for payment —
    immigration fraud is a serious problem and can jeopardise your application.
  </p>

